ormalsize<br>&gt; \end{center}<br>&gt; \vspace{0.2in}<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; 	extbf{Problem 1: Reading data into R}<br>&gt;  egin{enumerate}<br>&gt; \item Download the file &quot;WomenStats.txt&quot; from the course website and read<br>&gt; this into R using<br>&gt; read.table(), calling the new R object \verb+women+.<br>&gt; \item What is the class and dimension of the object  \verb+women+?<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem1&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; #women&lt;-read.table(&quot;WomenStats.txt&quot;, header=TRUE)<br>&gt; xd1 &lt;-<br>&gt; &quot;height  weight    age<br>&gt; 58         115    33<br>&gt; 59         117    34<br>&gt; 60         120    37<br>&gt; 61         123    31<br>&gt; 62         126    31<br>&gt; 63         129    34<br>&gt; 64         132    31<br>&gt; 65         135    39<br>&gt; 66         139    35<br>&gt; 67         142    34<br>&gt; 68         146    34<br>&gt; 69         150    36<br>&gt; 70         154    33<br>&gt; 71         159    30<br>&gt; 72         164    37<br>&gt; 71         160    NA<br>&gt; 73         161    35&quot;<br>&gt; women &lt;- read.table (textConnection(xd1), header=TRUE)<br>&gt; class(women)<br>&gt; dim(women)<br>&gt; summary(women)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; The \Rfunction{summary} is useful and it tells us that there is 1 missing<br>&gt; values (NA) in the age column.  We will need to keep this in mind when we<br>&gt; apply some of our statistical analyses on the data.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \end{enumerate}<br>&gt;<br>&gt; 
ewpage<br>&gt;<br>&gt; 	extbf{Problem 2: Matrix manipulations}<br>&gt;  egin{enumerate}<br>&gt; \item A new woman joined the study, she is 66&quot; tall, 165 lbs and is 34<br>&gt; years. Use \Rfunction{rbind} to append a row, containing her information to<br>&gt; \verb+women+<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; newWoman&lt;-c(66, 165,34)<br>&gt; women&lt;-rbind(women, newWoman)<br>&gt; summary(women)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \item How many women have a weight under 140?<br>&gt;<br>&gt; You can simple get a vector of TRUE/FALSE by subsetting the women whose<br>&gt; weight is less than 140.  The function \Rfunction{sum} can be used to<br>&gt; quickly and easily count the number which are TRUE<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.1&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; women$weight&lt;140<br>&gt; sum(women$weight&lt;140)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Alternative, create a vector and either tabulate it using \Rfunction{table}<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.1.1&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; table(women$weight&lt;140)<br>&gt;<br>&gt; weight140 &lt;- ifelse(women$weight&lt;140, &quot;under140&quot;, &quot;over140&quot;)<br>&gt; table(weight140)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \item What is the average height of women who weigh between 135 and 145<br>&gt; pounds  (hint: first select the data and then find the mean. See the section<br>&gt; in lecture 1 on Boolean terms and subsetting).<br>&gt;<br>&gt; First, lets subset the women who weight between 135 and 145 pounds and save<br>&gt; this index.  Then select the height column and these rows from the matrix.<br>&gt; I show a number of ways to do this below.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.2.1&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; index&lt;- women$weight&gt;135 &amp; women$weight&lt;145<br>&gt; index<br>&gt; dim(women[index, ])<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; There are two women within this weight range. Now get the mean height of<br>&gt; these two women.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.2.2&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt;<br>&gt; mean(women[index, 1])<br>&gt; mean(women[index, &quot;height&quot;])<br>&gt; mean(women$height[index])<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Another way to do this subsetting and selection is to use the function<br>&gt; \Rfunction{subset}<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.2.3&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; help(subset)<br>&gt; subWomen&lt;-subset(women, subset=women$weight&gt;135 &amp; women$weight&lt;145,<br>&gt; select=height)<br>&gt; print(subWomen)<br>&gt; colMeans(subWomen)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \item Get help on the command \verb+colnames+<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.3&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; ?colnames<br>&gt; help(colnames)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \item Change the rownames of \verb+women+ to the letters of the alphabet, eg<br>&gt; &quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;, &quot;D&quot; etc<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.4&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; rownames(women) &lt;- LETTERS[1:nrow(women)]<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \item There is a correction to the women is row  D, her age should be 39.<br>&gt; Change the age in row &quot;D&quot; to 39<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.5&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; women[&quot;D&quot;,]<br>&gt; women[&quot;D&quot;, &quot;age&quot;] &lt;-39<br>&gt; women[&quot;D&quot;,]<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \item Sort the matrix \verb+women+ by &#39;weight&#39; and store the result in<br>&gt; \verb+newWomen+<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem2.6&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; newWomen&lt;-women[order(women$weight),]<br>&gt; newWomen<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \end{enumerate}<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; 
ewpage<br>&gt; 	extbf{Problem 3: Using Apply}<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;  egin{itemize}<br>&gt; \item Use apply to generate a summary report, with the mean, median, sd of<br>&gt; height, weight and age. \ (Hint: use the \verb+apply+ function to get the<br>&gt; \verb+mean+, \verb+median+ and \verb+sd+ of the columns and use \verb+rbind+<br>&gt; to create a matrix with rownames; mean, median and sd).<br>&gt;<br>&gt; The \Rfunction{summary} only provides the mean, median, min, max and IQR of<br>&gt; each column. To get the standard deviation, or variance we can use apply to<br>&gt; get the \Rfunction{sd} \Rfunction{var} of each column.  By default it shows<br>&gt; 6 decimal places.  For display purposes, we wish to see only 2 decimal<br>&gt; places.<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem3&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; summary(women)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;note3.1&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; colsd&lt;-apply(women,2, sd)<br>&gt; signif(colsd, digits=3)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Note: because of the NA (missing value) in Age, we get NA when we try to<br>&gt; calculate the mean of age. So we need to give age an extra parameter so it<br>&gt; knows how to deal with missing values.  We can tell mean to ignore missing<br>&gt; values by setting the parameter na.rm =TRUE.  There are 2 ways to pass<br>&gt; parameters to a function when using apply. One is writing it after the<br>&gt; function call, there other is to create a function within the apply call.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;note3.2&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; apply(women,2, mean)<br>&gt; apply(women,2, mean, na.rm=TRUE)<br>&gt; apply(women,2, function(x) mean(x,na.rm=TRUE))<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; We could pass more than one argument to the function using either of these<br>&gt; approaches. For example if we also wished to trim 10% of observation from<br>&gt; each end of the mean.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;note3.3&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; apply(women,2, mean, na.rm=TRUE, trim=0.1)<br>&gt; apply(women,2, function(x) mean(x,na.rm=TRUE, trim=.1))<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; So here is the function<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Function3.4&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt;<br>&gt; mySummary&lt;-function(df) {<br>&gt;     colMeans&lt;-apply(df,2, mean, na.rm=TRUE)<br>&gt;     colMedians&lt;-apply(df,2, median, na.rm=TRUE)<br>&gt;     colSDs&lt;-apply(df,2, sd, na.rm=TRUE)<br>&gt;     res&lt;-rbind(mean=colMeans, median=colMedians, sd=colSDs)<br>&gt;     res&lt;-apply(res,2, signif, digits = 3)<br>&gt;     return(res)<br>&gt;     }<br>&gt;<br>&gt; mySummary(women)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \end{itemize}<br>&gt;<br>&gt; 	extbf{Problem 4: Loops and Functions}<br>&gt;  egin{enumerate}<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \item  Write a function to calculate BMI. The function should have 2 inputs;<br>&gt; weight(lb) and height(in) and should return one value; BMI.<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem4.1&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt;<br>&gt; bmi&lt;-function(weight, height) {<br>&gt;     bmi&lt;- (weight/height^2)*703<br>&gt;     bmi&lt;-  round(bmi, 2)<br>&gt;     return(bmi)<br>&gt;     }<br>&gt;<br>&gt; bmi(weight=150, height=65)<br>&gt; bmi(150, 65)<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \item  Do the women have a BMI within the recommend range ? (Normal<br>&gt; $\approx$  18.5-24.9)?<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Here is how we would calculate the BMI of every woman.<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem4.2.1&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; bmi(women$weight, women$height)<br>&gt; cat(paste(&quot;The BMI of woman&quot;, rownames(women),&quot;is&quot;, bmi(women$weight,<br>&gt; women$height), &quot;
&quot;))<br>&gt;<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; To test the output of the calculation to see if it falls within normal<br>&gt; range.  We will do this using a few alternative approaches (see below)<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Solution I would use.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem4.3.3&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; bmiRes&lt;-bmi(women$weight, women$height)<br>&gt; women&lt;-cbind(women, bmi=bmiRes)<br>&gt; res&lt;-sum(women$bmi&lt;18.5 | women$bmi&gt;24.9)<br>&gt; print(paste(&quot;The number of women with BMI values outside the normal range<br>&gt; is:&quot;, res))<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Alternative 1 : Using a For loop.  This is not the optimal solution, but I<br>&gt; include it here so you would see how you could write a For loop<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem4.3.1&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt;<br>&gt; for (i in 1:nrow(women))  {<br>&gt;      bmiRes&lt;-bmi(women$weight[i], women$height[i])<br>&gt;      out&lt;-ifelse(bmiRes&lt;18.5 | bmiRes&gt;24.9, &quot;Not normal&quot;, &quot;normal&quot;)<br>&gt;      print(out)<br>&gt;      }<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Alternative 2<br>&gt; Saving the output to a vector.  Again using a For loop.  This is not the<br>&gt; optimal solution, but I include it here so you would see how to add values<br>&gt; to an empty vector when using a For loop<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem4.3.2&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt;<br>&gt; BMIres&lt;-vector()<br>&gt;<br>&gt; for (i in 1:nrow(women))  {<br>&gt;      bmiRes[i]&lt;-bmi(women$weight[i], women$height[i])<br>&gt;      }<br>&gt;<br>&gt; print(bmiRes)<br>&gt; print(paste(&quot;Number of abnormal bmi values: &quot;, sum(bmiRes&lt;18.5 |<br>&gt; bmiRes&gt;24.9)))<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Taking the vector above, but printing the result using an ifelse conditional<br>&gt; test<br>&gt;<br>&gt; &lt;&lt;Problem4.3.3&gt;&gt;=<br>&gt; res&lt;- ifelse(bmiRes&lt;18.5 | bmiRes&gt;24.9, &quot;abnormal BMI&quot;, &quot;normal BMI&quot;)<br>&gt; table(res)<br>&gt; \end{enumerate}<br>&gt; @<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \end{enumerate}<br>&gt;<br>&gt; \end{document}<br>
